::ENG3_U04_L01_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: In the poem, the speaker returns to a closed mill and notices "dust on the machines like gray snow." The speaker says the town still hears the mill bell even though the bell has not rung for years. Which research question is most focused and interpretive? {
~What is a mill?#This is a factual question, not a literary research question.
=How does the image of the silent mill bell develop the idea that industrial change leaves emotional traces?#Correct. This connects a textual detail to an interpretation.
~How many mills exist in the world?#This is too broad and not tied to the passage.
~Why do people write poems?#This is too broad for this text.
}

::ENG3_U04_L01_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: In the poem, the speaker returns to a closed mill and notices "dust on the machines like gray snow." The speaker says the town still hears the mill bell even though the bell has not rung for years. Which detail would best support a research question about memory? {
~The speaker returns to a closed mill.#This could matter, but it is less directly connected to memory than the bell that is still heard.
~The machines have dust.#This detail supports abandonment, but memory is clearer in the remembered sound.
=The town still hears the mill bell even though it has not rung for years.#Correct. This detail directly suggests memory and lingering impact.
~The poem has a speaker.#This is true but too general.
}

::ENG3_U04_L01_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: In the poem, the speaker returns to a closed mill and notices "dust on the machines like gray snow." The speaker says the town still hears the mill bell even though the bell has not rung for years. Which question is too broad? {
~How does the silent bell represent memory in the poem?#This is focused and interpretive.
~How does the dust image contribute to the poem's mood?#This is focused on one textual detail.
~How does the closed mill shape the speaker's view of change?#This is tied to the passage.
=What is change?#Correct. This question is too broad and not tied to the text.
}

::ENG3_U04_L01_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: In the poem, the speaker returns to a closed mill and notices "dust on the machines like gray snow." The speaker says the town still hears the mill bell even though the bell has not rung for years. Which phrase uses academic vocabulary most precisely? {
=The question has an interpretive focus because it connects the bell image to memory.#Correct. This uses precise research vocabulary.
~The question is about stuff in the poem.#This is vague.
~The poem thing has a meaning maybe.#This is unclear and imprecise.
~The research is cool because bells are cool.#This is informal and not academic.
}

::ENG3_U04_L01_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: In the poem, the speaker returns to a closed mill and notices "dust on the machines like gray snow." The speaker says the town still hears the mill bell even though the bell has not rung for years. What makes a research question useful for this passage? {
~It asks only for a dictionary definition.#That would not guide literary interpretation.
~It ignores the poem and asks about mills in general.#A useful question stays connected to the text.
=It connects a specific textual detail to a possible interpretation.#Correct. This is the goal of a focused literary research question.
~It can be answered with yes or no.#Yes/no questions are usually too limited for research.
}
